; regression test: the lexer used to merge adjacent "standard operator" punctuation greedily,
; and bracket characters (`{`/`}`/`[`/`]`) were part of that same greedy class -- so
; `{0, 1, 2, ...}` (no space before the closing brace) lexed `...}` as *one* token, silently
; swallowing the closing brace. The consequence was worse than a parse error: the resulting
; unclosed bracket left the parser permanently "waiting for a continuation", and
; `read_eval_loop` just breaks out of its loop on EOF while in that state -- silently
; discarding the *rest of the file*, no error at all. This is exactly what happened to
; `natural.kurt` (doc/kurt-soundness.md #7) before anyone noticed, since nothing ever
; `load`ed it. Fixed by giving bracket characters their own always-single-char alternative in
; the scanner, matching how `(`/`)` already worked. Confirmed here with no workaround space --
; if this regresses, `true` below is silently never reached and the file fails to close.
brackets { }
parse {0, 1, 2, ...}
true
;;; Proof checked.
